"Internal Error ()\nciteturn3046view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.13255v1\",\"lineno\":16}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.13255v1: Cache miss\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2510.13271v1] Do You Get the Hint? Benchmarking LLMs on the Board Game Concept (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.13271v1)\nciteturn3046view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.13271v1\",\"lineno\":16}); Total lines: 160\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 15 Oct 2025 (this version), latest version 6 Jan 2026 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:Do You Get the Hint? Benchmarking LLMs on the Board Game Concept\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Ine Gevers , cite7†Walter Daelemans L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Do You Get the Hint? Benchmarking LLMs on the Board Game Concept, by Ine Gevers and 1 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF cite9†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Large language models (LLMs) have achieved striking successes on many benchmarks, yet recent studies continue to expose fundamental weaknesses. In particular, tasks that require abstract reasoning remain challenging, often because they use representations such as grids, symbols, or visual patterns that differ from the natural language data LLMs are trained on.\nL17: In this paper, we introduce Concept, a simple word-guessing board game, as a benchmark for probing abductive reasoning in a representation that is much closer to LLM pre-training data: natural language. Our results show that this game, easily solved by humans (with a success rate of over 90\\%), is still very challenging for state-of-the-art LLMs (no model exceeds 40\\% success rate).\nL18: Specifically, we observe that LLMs struggle with interpreting other players' strategic intents, and with correcting initial hypotheses given sequential information updates. In addition, we extend the evaluation across multiple languages, and find that the LLM performance drops further in lower-resource languages (Dutch, French, and Spanish) compared to English.\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:2510.13271 [cs.CL]\nL21:    | (or cite11†arXiv:2510.13271v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL22:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.13271†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Ine Gevers [cite13†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Wed, 15 Oct 2025 08:17:25 UTC (280 KB)\nL27: cite5†[v2] Tue, 6 Jan 2026 08:35:38 UTC (348 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled Do You Get the Hint? Benchmarking LLMs on the Board Game Concept, by Ine Gevers and 1 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite8†View PDF L36:   * cite9†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite14†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite15†view license†creativecommons.org L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.CL\nL43: \nL44: cite16†< prev |   cite17†next > L45: \nL46: cite18†new | cite19†recent | cite20†2025-10 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite21†cs L51: \nL52: ### References & Citations\nL53: \nL54:   * cite22†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L55:   * cite23†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L56:   * cite24†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L57: \nL58: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL59: \nL60: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL61: \nL62: [Button: ×]\nL63: \nL64: loading...\nL65: \nL66: Data provided by:\nL67: \nL68: ### Bookmark\nL69: \nL70: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\n